            {
              "id": "CVE-2019-3901",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: perf_event_open() and execve() race in setuid programs allows a data leak"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A race condition in perf_event_open() allows local attackers to leak sensitive data from setuid programs. As no relevant locks (in particular the cred_guard_mutex) are held during the ptrace_may_access() call, it is possible for the specified target task to perform an execve() syscall with setuid execution before perf_event_alloc() actually attaches to it, allowing an attacker to bypass the ptrace_may_access() check and the perf_event_exit_task(current) call that is performed in install_exec_creds() during privileged execve() calls. This issue affects kernel versions before 4.8."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2019-3901",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2019-3901\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: 3.16.68-1\nLink: [CVE-2019-3901](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2019-3901)\nA race condition in perf_event_open() allows local attackers to leak sensitive data from setuid programs. As no relevant locks (in particular the cred_guard_mutex) are held during the ptrace_may_access() call, it is possible for the specified target task to perform an execve() syscall with setuid execution before perf_event_alloc() actually attaches to it, allowing an attacker to bypass the ptrace_may_access() check and the perf_event_exit_task(current) call that is performed in install_exec_creds() during privileged execve() calls. This issue affects kernel versions before 4.8.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2019-3901**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev|3.16.68-1|[CVE-2019-3901](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2019-3901)|\n\nA race condition in perf_event_open() allows local attackers to leak sensitive data from setuid programs. As no relevant locks (in particular the cred_guard_mutex) are held during the ptrace_may_access() call, it is possible for the specified target task to perform an execve() syscall with setuid execution before perf_event_alloc() actually attaches to it, allowing an attacker to bypass the ptrace_may_access() check and the perf_event_exit_task(current) call that is performed in install_exec_creds() during privileged execve() calls. This issue affects kernel versions before 4.8."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cvssv2_score": 1.9,
