            {
              "id": "CVE-2020-10690",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: use-after-free in cdev_put() when a PTP device is removed while it's chardev is open"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "There is a use-after-free in kernel versions before 5.5 due to a race condition between the release of ptp_clock and cdev while resource deallocation. When a (high privileged) process allocates a ptp device file (like /dev/ptpX) and voluntarily goes to sleep. During this time if the underlying device is removed, it can cause an exploitable condition as the process wakes up to terminate and clean all attached files. The system crashes due to the cdev structure being invalid (as already freed) which is pointed to by the inode."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2020-10690",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2020-10690\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: 3.16.84-1\nLink: [CVE-2020-10690](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2020-10690)\nThere is a use-after-free in kernel versions before 5.5 due to a race condition between the release of ptp_clock and cdev while resource deallocation. When a (high privileged) process allocates a ptp device file (like /dev/ptpX) and voluntarily goes to sleep. During this time if the underlying device is removed, it can cause an exploitable condition as the process wakes up to terminate and clean all attached files. The system crashes due to the cdev structure being invalid (as already freed) which is pointed to by the inode.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2020-10690**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev|3.16.84-1|[CVE-2020-10690](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2020-10690)|\n\nThere is a use-after-free in kernel versions before 5.5 due to a race condition between the release of ptp_clock and cdev while resource deallocation. When a (high privileged) process allocates a ptp device file (like /dev/ptpX) and voluntarily goes to sleep. During this time if the underlying device is removed, it can cause an exploitable condition as the process wakes up to terminate and clean all attached files. The system crashes due to the cdev structure being invalid (as already freed) which is pointed to by the inode."
              },
