            {
              "id": "CVE-2019-6111",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "openssh: Improper validation of object names allows malicious server to overwrite files via scp client"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An issue was discovered in OpenSSH 7.9. Due to the scp implementation being derived from 1983 rcp, the server chooses which files/directories are sent to the client. However, the scp client only performs cursory validation of the object name returned (only directory traversal attacks are prevented). A malicious scp server (or Man-in-The-Middle attacker) can overwrite arbitrary files in the scp client target directory. If recursive operation (-r) is performed, the server can manipulate subdirectories as well (for example, to overwrite the .ssh/authorized_keys file)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2019-6111",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2019-6111\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: openssh-client\nFixed Version: 1:6.7p1-5+deb8u8\nLink: [CVE-2019-6111](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2019-6111)\nAn issue was discovered in OpenSSH 7.9. Due to the scp implementation being derived from 1983 rcp, the server chooses which files/directories are sent to the client. However, the scp client only performs cursory validation of the object name returned (only directory traversal attacks are prevented). A malicious scp server (or Man-in-The-Middle attacker) can overwrite arbitrary files in the scp client target directory. If recursive operation (-r) is performed, the server can manipulate subdirectories as well (for example, to overwrite the .ssh/authorized_keys file).",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2019-6111**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|openssh-client|1:6.7p1-5+deb8u8|[CVE-2019-6111](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2019-6111)|\n\nAn issue was discovered in OpenSSH 7.9. Due to the scp implementation being derived from 1983 rcp, the server chooses which files/directories are sent to the client. However, the scp client only performs cursory validation of the object name returned (only directory traversal attacks are prevented). A malicious scp server (or Man-in-The-Middle attacker) can overwrite arbitrary files in the scp client target directory. If recursive operation (-r) is performed, the server can manipulate subdirectories as well (for example, to overwrite the .ssh/authorized_keys file)."
              },
